.TH TEST 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
test, [ \- evaluate a conditional expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B test
.RI [ expression ]
.br
.B [
.RI [ expression ]
.B ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B test
evaluates
.I expression
and exits with status 0 if it is true and 1 if it is false.
With no expression the result is false.
.PP
When the program is invoked under the name
.B [
(the last component of
.I argv[0]
is compared), the final argument must be
.BR ] ,
which is then discarded.
The Substrate build installs only
.IR /bin/test ;
no
.B [
link is created, so the
.B [
form requires a link made by other means or a shell builtin.
.PP
The expression is parsed by recursive descent.
When the token after the current one is a binary operator, the three
tokens are taken as a binary expression; otherwise a recognised unary
operator followed by an argument is a unary expression; otherwise a
single argument is true if it is a non-empty string.
.SS File operators
.TP
.BI \-e " file"
.I file
exists (tested with
.BR access (2)).
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
.TQ
.BI \-w " file"
.TQ
.BI \-x " file"
.I file
is readable, writable, or executable by the real user ID, as reported
by
.BR access (2).
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Regular file.
.TP
.BI \-d " file"
Directory.
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Character device.
.TP
.BI \-b " file"
Block device.
.TP
.BI \-p " file"
FIFO.
.TP
.BI \-S " file"
Socket.
.TP
.BI \-L " file"
.TQ
.BI \-h " file"
Symbolic link (not followed).
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Size is greater than zero.
.TP
.BI \-g " file"
.TQ
.BI \-u " file"
.TQ
.BI \-k " file"
Set-group-ID, set-user-ID, or sticky bit is set.
.TP
.BI \-t " fd"
File descriptor
.I fd
is a terminal.
.PP
All file operators except
.BR \-L " and " \-h
follow symbolic links.
.SS String operators
.TP
.BI \-n " string"
.I string
is non-empty.
.TP
.BI \-z " string"
.I string
is empty.
.TP
.I string
.I string
is non-empty.
.TP
.IB s1 " = " s2
.TQ
.IB s1 " != " s2
The strings are identical, or differ.
.SS Integer operators
.TP
.IB n1 " \-eq " n2
.TQ
.IB n1 " \-ne " n2
.TQ
.IB n1 " \-lt " n2
.TQ
.IB n1 " \-le " n2
.TQ
.IB n1 " \-gt " n2
.TQ
.IB n1 " \-ge " n2
Signed decimal integer comparison.
An operand that is not a complete decimal integer in the range of a
.B long
is a syntax error.
.SS Combinators
In order of increasing precedence:
.TP
.IB expr1 " \-o " expr2
Logical OR.
.TP
.IB expr1 " \-a " expr2
Logical AND.
.TP
.BI ! " expr"
Negation.
.TP
.BI ( " expr " )
Grouping.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The expression is true.
.TP
.B 1
The expression is false, or no expression was given.
.TP
.B 2
Syntax error, invalid integer, unknown operator, extra argument, or a
missing
.B ]
when invoked as
.BR [ .
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ test \-d /tmp && echo directory
$ test "$a" = "$b" \-o \-z "$c"
$ test 3 \-lt 10; echo $?
0
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR access (2),
.BR stat (2),
.BR isatty (3)
.SH STANDARDS
Intended to follow POSIX.1-2024
.BR test ,
with
.BR \-a ", " \-o ", " ( ", " ) ", " \-h ", " \-k
as the common XSI and BSD extensions.
.SH BUGS
The file comparison operators
.BR \-nt ,
.B \-ot
and
.B \-ef
are recognised as binary operators but not implemented: they are
evaluated as integer comparisons and fail with a syntax error.
.PP
The POSIX rules that select a parse by argument count are not applied,
so a leading
.B !
or
.B (
is always taken as an operator.
For example,
.B test ! = !
is reported as a syntax error instead of comparing two strings.
